# Logrotate

Example configuration: /etc/logrotate.d/demo-app
/var/log/demo-app/app-01.log {
    missingok
    compress
    daily
    copytruncate
    rotate 7
    notifempty
    dateext
}

## Log Rotation Rules for demo-app
- The rule applies to the demo-app application log.
- The log file is stored at /var/log/demo-app/app-01.log.
- Logrotate checks this rule daily and rotates the log when the rotation conditions are met.
- Up to seven rotated log files are retained.
- Rotated files are compressed.
- The rotation date is added to the archive filename.
- If the log file is missing, logrotate does not report an error and skips it.
- Empty log files are not rotated.
- The copytruncate option allows the application to continue writing to the original log file after rotation. Logrotate copies the current log contents to an archive and then truncates the original file without replacing it.

## Verification
Check the logrotate configuration without making changes:
    sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/demo-app
Force log rotation:
    sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/demo-app
Check the resulting files:
    ls -l /var/log/demo-app/
Verify that the application continues writing to the original log file after rotation.
In the first terminal, monitor the application log:
    tail -f /var/log/demo-app/app-01.log
In a second terminal, trigger an application action that generates a log entry.
Check that the new entry appears in the original log file.
Press Ctrl+C to stop monitoring.